course: dictionary
lesson: 1290
title: Butty: one word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one part of the country or one kind of talk. Context helps you find their meaning. «In north-west England a butty is a sandwich, and "our kid" means my brother or sister, or a close mate.» The sentence tells you the meaning straight away: a butty is a sandwich.
explain: Clues in the words near a word can help too. «I'll have a bacon butty, please.» Bacon is a food, so a butty is probably a sandwich. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: «I'll have a bacon sandwich, please» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. In Wales, a butty can mean a friend or workmate. When you meet butty, read the rest of the sentence. If it talks about food, it is a sandwich. If it talks about people, it may mean a friend.
fact: A butty is a sandwich, often with a hot filling.
fact: «Chip butty» means chips in buttered bread.
fact: «Bacon butty» means a bacon sandwich.
fact: In Wales, butty can mean a friend or workmate.
fact: Butty is informal and is most common in northern England, so a butty tells the reader the speaker is probably from the north.
exercise: «I'll have a chip butty, please.» What is a chip butty? | A bowl of soup with chips on the side | Chips in a cup of tea | A hot drink with a biscuit | *Chips in buttered bread
exercise: Mum asks, «Jam butty for tea?» What is a jam butty? | A pot of hot jam | A jar of jam | *A jam sandwich | A cake with jam on top
exercise: Ellis says, «That's my butty from work.» What does butty mean here? | A kind of tea | A hot sandwich | A bread roll | *A friend or workmate
test: In north-west England, what is a butty? | A boat | A pair of shoes | *A sandwich | A kind of dance
test: Which phrase means a bacon sandwich? | A bacon bath | A bacon car | *A bacon butty | A bacon letter
exam: Which sentence uses butty to mean a friend? | «He has a butty in his bag.» | *«Me and my butty are going to the match.» | «I ate a jam butty for lunch.» | «The butty fell off the table.»
